  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 203 PM CDT Sat Oct 31 2026\n\nAnother shortwave is moving out of the TX/OK Panhandles into Kansas \nwith renewed moisture transport ahead of it. This supports another \nperiod of scattered showers this evening/overnight. Instability is\nminimal, so the threat for thunderstorms is very low. \n\nTemperatures through Saturday stay on the mild side of normal\nbeneath persistent cloud cover and southerly low\u2011level flow.\nHighs will generally be in the upper 60s to lower 70s and lows in \nthe mid 50s.\n\nMid\u2011level ridging will briefly build in over the central Plains on \nSunday behind the departing shortwave. Low\u2011level flow remains \nsoutherly, and the air mass stays moist, but large\u2011scale ascent is \nweaker, so Sunday\u2019s forecast trends toward mostly dry for much of \nthe area, but isolated showers can't be ruled out. \n\nAttention then turns to a deeper, more amplified trough digging into\nthe High Plains, with lee cyclogenesis and a strengthening surface \nlow forecast to move quickly into north\u2011central Kansas Monday. A \nrobust 50\u201360 kt low\u2011level jet will develop ahead of a rapidly \nadvancing cold front with surface wind gusts around 40 mph likely. \nInstability appears marginal, but shear will be ample given the \nhighly dynamic nature of the system. The overall severe threat will \nbe sensitive to the degree of cloud cover, morning/early afternoon \nrainfall and timing of the front. If cloud cover and rain clears out \nby the afternoon then we could see enough destabilization for \nstronger storms. Given the shear profile, all severe threats would be\npossible in any super cells that develop. However, the more \nconsistent signal is for cloud cover and scattered showers/storms to \nlinger long enough to inhibit clearing and destabilization, leading \nto more of a heavy rain threat than anything else. With PWATs once \nagain in record territory, rain rates in the 2-3 inch an hour range \nwill be likely. \n\nAs the upper low slows or becomes partially cut off from the main\nwesterlies Tuesday, showers may persist, especially east of Highway\n75, with cooler air advecting in behind the front. Highs on Tuesday\ntrend back into the 60s, with lows in the 40s to lower 50s by\nTuesday night. Confidence is moderate in the presence of showers,\nbut lower in their exact coverage and duration, given model\ndifferences in the evolution of the cutoff low.\n\nBy mid\u2011week, ensemble guidance suggests the larger\u2011scale trough will\ngradually shift east and heights will flatten over the Plains, \nallowing for a slow drying trend. However, model spread increases \nafter Tuesday regarding how quickly the upper low exits and whether \nadditional shortwaves rotate through the region. Temperatures should  \nsettle into a more seasonal range: highs mid\u201160s and lows mid\u201140s.",
